0.0006556510925292969, -0.0030193328857421875, 0.004718780517578125, 0.01224517822265625, -0.007297515869140625, 0.0009202957153320312, -0.02685546875, -0.00896453857421875, 0.032012939453125, 0.022003173828125, 0.0204925537109375, -0.0145111083984375, -0.0014057159423828125, 0.01001739501953125, 0.01727294921875, -0.01399993896484375, 0.0097808837890625, -0.006465911865234375, -0.0033283233642578125, 0.02294921875, 0.0047607421875, 0.0029773712158203125, 0.005817413330078125, 0.03350830078125, 0.0297088623046875, -0.0003299713134765625, -0.016693115234375, -0.006740570068359375, 0.00733184814453125, -0.004123687744140625, 0.01183319091796875, 0.008758544921875, -0.01172637939453125, 0.0014286041259765625, -0.01483917236328125, -0.0224609375, -0.00879669189453125, -0.01398468017578125, 0.016265869140625, -0.05426025390625, 0.01458740234375, -0.01300811767578125, -0.02398681640625, 0.0035915374755859375, -0.033477783203125, 0.007389068603515625, -0.0095672607421875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. However, the reliability of these methods\u2014defined here as the consistency and predictability of agent performance across stochastic environments and task orderings\u2014has been critically overlooked. In this work, we address this gap by systematically evaluating the robustness of memory-based self-improving agents and proposing interventions to improve their reliability. We also introduce oversight mechanisms, defined as processes or tools that enable transparent monitoring and auditing of agent memory and behavior.\n\n**Research Questions and Hypotheses**\n\nThis study is guided by the following research questions:\n1. How robust and reliable are existing memory-based self-improving agents when evaluated across stochastic environments and randomized task orderings?\n2. Can the integration of structured rubrics and explicit environment feedback into agent memory reduce performance variance and improve reliability?\n3. Do standardized evaluation protocols and human-auditable oversight mechanisms enhance the transparency and reproducibility of agent performance?\n\nWe hypothesize that (a) existing agents exhibit substantial performance variance (fragility) across runs and task orderings, (b) specification interventions (structured rubrics and environment feedback) will reduce this variance and improve reliability, and (c) oversight mechanisms and standardized protocols will further enhance transparency and reproducibility.\n\nOur main findings reveal that (1) existing memory-based agents exhibit substantial performance variance across runs and task orderings, indicating fragility; (2) integrating structured rubrics and explicit environment feedback into memory entries significantly reduces this variance and improves reliability; (3) standardized evaluation protocols and human-auditable oversight interfaces further enhance transparency and reproducibility. These results highlight the importance of robust memory construction and transparent evaluation for deploying self-improving agents in real-world, stochastic settings.\n\n**1. Introduction and Scope**\n\nRecent advances in memory-based self-improving agents (e.g., Smith et al., 2022; Lee & Gupta, 2023) have demonstrated the potential of textual memory banks for continual learning. However, prior work has primarily focused on average performance, with limited attention to the consistency of results across stochastic factors such as random seeds and task orderings. Our work is the first to (a) systematically quantify robustness and fragility in these agents using variance-based metrics, (b) introduce and empirically validate specification interventions (structured rubrics and environment feedback) for memory construction, (c) propose and evaluate standardized evaluation protocols for reproducibility, and (d) design and assess human-auditable oversight mechanisms for agent memory. Unlike previous studies, we explicitly measure performance variance, analyze memory content quality, and provide transparent, reproducible oversight tools.\n\nIn summary, our contributions provide actionable insights for improving the reliability and transparency of memory-based self-improving agents, with implications for their deployment in safety-critical or unpredictable environments.\n\n**2. Key Terms and Operational Definitions**\n\nTo avoid redundancy, we summarize key terms with single definitions and examples. We clarify the distinctions as follows:\n\n| Term         | Definition                                                                 | Example                                                                                   |\n|--------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Reliability  | The degree to which an agent\u2019s performance is consistent and predictable across repeated runs and different task orderings. Reliability is a holistic property reflecting both robustness (low variance) and low fragility (low sensitivity to perturbations). | An agent consistently achieves similar task completion rates regardless of random seed or task sequence. |\n| Robustness   | The extent to which agent performance remains stable under controlled perturbations, such as changes in random seed or task order. Robustness is quantitatively measured by low variance (e.g., standard deviation) in performance metrics across these perturbations. | Performance variance (e.g., standard deviation) remains low when task order or environment changes. |\n| Fragility    | The degree to which agent performance is highly sensitive to stochastic factors or task order, resulting in large increases in performance variance. High fragility is the converse of robustness and undermines reliability. | Agent\u2019s completion rate drops sharply when task order is shuffled or environment is perturbed. |\n\n**3. Main Contributions and Novelty**\n\nOur main contributions, and how they advance beyond prior work, are as follows:\n\n1. **Systematic Robustness Evaluation:**  \n   - We provide the first comprehensive quantification of robustness and fragility in memory-based self-improving agents by measuring performance variance (standard deviation, confidence intervals) across multiple independent runs and randomized task orderings. Prior work (Smith et al., 2022; Lee & Gupta, 2023) reported only mean performance, without variance or sensitivity analyses.\n\n2. **Specification Interventions:**  \n   - We introduce automated integration of structured rubrics and explicit environment feedback into agent memory entries. Unlike previous studies, which relied on unstructured or loosely formatted memory, our interventions use template-based extraction and rule-based parsing to standardize and enrich memory content, directly targeting sources of fragility. These interventions are designed to make memory entries more precise, actionable, and less prone to ambiguity, thereby improving the agent\u2019s ability to generalize and perform reliably across varying conditions.\n\n3. **Standardized Evaluation Protocols:**  \n   - We propose and empirically validate protocols requiring results to be reported across multiple runs and randomized curricula, using standardized benchmarks. This addresses reproducibility gaps in prior literature, where single-seed or fixed-order evaluations were common.\n\n4. **Oversight Mechanisms:**  \n   - We design and evaluate a human-auditable memory interface, enabling external reviewers to inspect, annotate, and edit agent memory. This interface provides features for filtering, searching, and annotating memory entries, and supports collaborative review. Prior work did not provide such tools or quantitative evaluation of oversight.\n\nThe following table summarizes how our interventions compare to prior work:\n\n| Aspect                        | Prior Work (Smith et al., 2022; Lee & Gupta, 2023) | This Work (Ours)                                   |\n|-------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|\n| Performance Reporting         | Mean only                                          | Mean, variance, confidence intervals               |\n| Memory Construction           | Unstructured, free-form                            | Structured rubrics, explicit environment feedback  |\n| Evaluation Protocols          | Single-seed, fixed order                           | Multiple runs, randomized curricula                |\n| Oversight Tools               | None                                               | Human-auditable, collaborative memory interface    |\n\n**4. Background and Methodological Details**\n\nTo provide context for our experimental methods, we briefly review the agent architectures and memory mechanisms under study.\n\nWe focus on two representative memory-based methods:\n\n- **Episodic Memory Augmented Agents (EMAA):** Agents store and retrieve textual summaries of past task experiences to inform future decisions. After each task, EMAA encodes a summary of the episode (e.g., actions taken, outcomes, and lessons learned) into a memory bank, which is then queried during subsequent tasks to guide behavior.\n- **Self-Reflective Memory Agents (SRMA):** Agents maintain a memory bank of self-generated improvement notes, such as explicit reflections on failures or strategies for improvement, generated by prompting themselves to reflect on their performance after each task. EMAA focuses on factual episodic recall, while SRMA emphasizes metacognitive self-reflection.\n\nBoth EMAA and SRMA are implemented following Smith et al. (2022) and Lee & Gupta (2023), respectively. For this study, we extend both methods to support automated integration of structured rubrics and environment feedback into memory entries (see Section 6). The memory retrieval module is adapted to handle the augmented memory format, and the memory bank size is fixed at 100 entries for all experiments. This size is chosen to balance memory capacity and computational tractability, consistent with prior work. However, we acknowledge that this fixed size may limit the agent\u2019s ability to store long-term knowledge or adapt to larger task suites, potentially affecting generalizability. Future work should explore the impact of varying memory bank sizes on agent performance and reliability.\n\n**5. Experimental Methods**\n\nTo comprehensively re-evaluate these methods, we broaden the scope of evaluation along two axes:  \n1. Including multiple runs to quantify variance  \n2. Randomly shuffling the tasks to investigate the effect of task order\n\nWe now describe the experimental setup and rationale for key choices.\n\n**Environments:**  \n- We conduct experiments in two simulated environments\u2014GridWorld and TextWorld.  \n  - *GridWorld*: A discrete, partially observable navigation environment with stochastic transitions and obstacles, requiring agents to plan and adapt to uncertainty.  \n  - *TextWorld*: A text-based interactive fiction environment with complex, multi-step tasks involving object manipulation, exploration, and language understanding.  \nBoth environments are widely used benchmarks for evaluating generalization, memory, and reasoning in agents, and their stochasticity and task diversity closely mirror challenges faced in real-world continual learning scenarios.\n\n**Task Suite and Agent Architecture:**  \n- We use a suite of 20 multi-step tasks per environment, spanning a range of difficulty levels from simple navigation to complex multi-object manipulation. This number is chosen to provide a balance between diversity and tractability, as in standard continual learning benchmarks (Smith et al., 2022; Lee & Gupta, 2023). While larger task suites could further test generalization, computational constraints limit our scope here. This limitation may affect the generalizability of our findings to more diverse or extensive task distributions.\n- For each method, we use a transformer-based agent architecture with 4 layers, 256 hidden units, and a memory retrieval module based on cosine similarity. Hyperparameters (learning rate 1e-4, batch size 16, memory bank size 100) are held constant across all experiments to ensure comparability and are chosen based on prior work and preliminary tuning for stable training. We note that these choices may limit generalizability to other architectures or hyperparameter regimes, and future work should investigate the robustness of our findings under different settings.\n- We perform 10 independent runs per task ordering, and for each run, we randomly shuffle the task sequence to assess sensitivity to curriculum."}}